# Day 06: Active Directory Automation

## Overview

On Day 06 of my PowerShell 10 Days: Zero to Hero challenge, I focused on Active Directory Automation using PowerShell on Windows Server 2022.

The goal of this practical session was to understand how PowerShell can help System Administrators manage Active Directory users, groups, and Organizational Units (OUs).

## Key Learning Areas

- Active Directory PowerShell Module
- Creating Active Directory Users
- Creating Security Groups
- Creating Organizational Units (OUs)
- Verifying Active Directory objects using PowerShell

## Hands-on Practical Exercises

### Lab 1 – Active Directory Module

Check whether the Active Directory PowerShell module is available and view its commands.

```powershell
Get-Module -ListAvailable ActiveDirectory

Get-Command -Module ActiveDirectory
```

### Lab 2 – Creating an Active Directory User

Retrieve domain information:

```powershell
Get-ADDomain
```

Create a test user:

```powershell
Import-Module ActiveDirectory

$domain = Get-ADDomain
$password = Read-Host "Enter test password" -AsSecureString

New-ADUser `
-Name "Lab User 01" `
-GivenName "Lab" `
-Surname "User" `
-SamAccountName "labuser01" `
-UserPrincipalName "labuser01@$($domain.DNSRoot)" `
-Path "CN=Users,$($domain.DistinguishedName)" `
-AccountPassword $password `
-Enabled $true
```

Verify the user:

```powershell
Get-ADUser labuser01 -Properties Enabled, UserPrincipalName |
Select-Object Name, SamAccountName, UserPrincipalName, Enabled
```

### Lab 3 – Creating an Active Directory Group

Create a Global Security Group:

```powershell
New-ADGroup `
-Name "IT-Support" `
-SamAccountName "IT-Support" `
-GroupCategory Security `
-GroupScope Global `
-Path "CN=Users,$((Get-ADDomain).DistinguishedName)"
```

Verify the group:

```powershell
Get-ADGroup IT-Support -Properties GroupScope, GroupCategory |
Select-Object Name, SamAccountName, GroupScope, GroupCategory
```

### Lab 4 – Creating an Organizational Unit (OU)

Create an Organizational Unit:

```powershell
New-ADOrganizationalUnit `
-Name "IT-Department" `
-Path (Get-ADDomain).DistinguishedName `
-ProtectedFromAccidentalDeletion $true
```

Verify the OU:

```powershell
Get-ADOrganizationalUnit -Filter 'Name -eq "IT-Department"' |
Select-Object Name, DistinguishedName
```

